1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:02,731
You press a key.

2
00:00:04,233 --> 00:00:05,991
It feels like nothing at all.

3
00:00:05,991 --> 00:00:08,052
A motion of barely one millimeter.

4
00:00:08,733 --> 00:00:12,955
But in the few hundredths of a second
before a letter appears on your screen,

5
00:00:12,955 --> 00:00:14,984
that press sets off a chain reaction.

6
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:19,051
Through layers of software,
written by thousands of engineers.

7
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:23,716
Through billions of transistors,
switching billions of times every second.

8
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:26,365
All the way down to a flow of electrons.

9
00:00:27,267 --> 00:00:29,091
Today, we follow that journey.

10
00:00:29,091 --> 00:00:30,125
All the way down.

11
00:00:39,733 --> 00:00:41,440
First, let's meet the machine.

12
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:46,861
Open up a modern Mac, and almost everything
that matters lives on one small chip.

13
00:00:47,267 --> 00:00:50,940
Apple silicon is a system
on a chip: a whole computer,

14
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:53,389
fused onto a single slab of silicon.

15
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:56,559
Performance cores and efficiency cores.

16
00:00:56,559 --> 00:00:58,026
A graphics processor.

17
00:00:58,026 --> 00:00:59,633
A Neural Engine for AI.

18
00:00:59,633 --> 00:01:02,988
Controllers for storage,
displays, and security.

19
00:01:02,988 --> 00:01:06,761
And right beside it, on the
same package — the memory.

20
00:01:07,267 --> 00:01:11,213
A chip like the M4 packs about
twenty-eight billion transistors.

21
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:15,052
But on its own, all that
hardware does nothing at all.

22
00:01:15,052 --> 00:01:16,103
It needs software.

23
00:01:17,433 --> 00:01:20,121
And software, it turns
out, is built in layers.

24
00:01:20,567 --> 00:01:25,112
At the very bottom sits the kernel
— the core of the operating system.

25
00:01:25,112 --> 00:01:29,463
On a Mac it's called XNU, and it
has total control of the hardware.

26
00:01:29,867 --> 00:01:33,062
Above it are system services,
like the WindowServer,

27
00:01:33,062 --> 00:01:35,520
which owns everything you see on screen.

28
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:39,237
Then come frameworks — like
AppKit, SwiftUI, and Metal —

29
00:01:39,237 --> 00:01:41,584
the building blocks apps are made from.

30
00:01:41,933 --> 00:01:43,555
And on top: your apps.

31
00:01:44,167 --> 00:01:48,484
Each layer talks only to the one
beneath it, through well-defined doors.

32
00:01:48,484 --> 00:01:53,340
That's abstraction — the trick that makes
something this complex possible at all.

33
00:01:53,867 --> 00:01:57,259
And one boundary is enforced
by the processor itself.

34
00:01:57,733 --> 00:01:59,199
Apps run in user mode.

35
00:01:59,199 --> 00:02:01,264
The kernel runs in kernel mode.

36
00:02:01,264 --> 00:02:05,461
Code on the outside can't touch
the hardware, or reach across —

37
00:02:05,461 --> 00:02:08,059
except through doors the kernel guards.

38
00:02:11,967 --> 00:02:13,396
So — back to that key.

39
00:02:13,833 --> 00:02:17,367
Under the keyboard is a grid
of wires — rows and columns —

40
00:02:17,367 --> 00:02:19,316
with a switch at every crossing.

41
00:02:19,667 --> 00:02:23,676
A small controller scans the grid
hundreds of times per second,

42
00:02:23,676 --> 00:02:27,304
powering one row at a time
and listening on every column.

43
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:30,567
Press a key, and you
connect a row to a column.

44
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:32,031
The controller spots it.

45
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:35,899
It waits a few milliseconds for
the contacts to stop bouncing…

46
00:02:36,133 --> 00:02:40,382
…then packs the press into a tiny
report, just a few bytes long.

47
00:02:40,382 --> 00:02:45,627
In HID — the common language of keyboards
— the letter A is simply number four.

48
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:49,279
Those bytes race to the chip,
where they trigger an interrupt.

49
00:02:50,367 --> 00:02:52,969
An interrupt is exactly
what it sounds like.

50
00:02:53,367 --> 00:02:56,234
A processor core drops
whatever it was doing.

51
00:02:56,234 --> 00:03:01,076
It saves its place, switches into kernel
mode, and jumps to a fixed address:

52
00:03:01,076 --> 00:03:02,988
the kernel's exception vector.

53
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:06,323
Within microseconds, the
kernel is in control.

54
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:11,683
Its drivers read the report, and turn
number four into an event: the key for A,

55
00:03:11,683 --> 00:03:12,503
pressed down.

56
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:15,344
That event travels up to the WindowServer.

57
00:03:15,344 --> 00:03:19,940
It knows which window is in front, so it
sends the key to the app that owns it.

58
00:03:21,067 --> 00:03:23,669
That app has been asleep,
waiting for a message.

59
00:03:24,033 --> 00:03:25,346
The kernel wakes it up.

60
00:03:25,346 --> 00:03:30,028
Its run loop picks up the event, and passes
it to the text field you're typing in.

61
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:36,871
Using your keyboard layout, the key
becomes a character: a lowercase a.

62
00:03:36,871 --> 00:03:40,881
In Unicode, that's
number 61 in hexadecimal.

63
00:03:40,881 --> 00:03:42,795
In binary: 0110 0001.

64
00:03:43,267 --> 00:03:47,158
To draw it, the text system pulls
the letter's shape from a font:

65
00:03:47,158 --> 00:03:49,133
a precise outline made of curves.

66
00:03:49,467 --> 00:03:53,072
The curves are filled in as
pixels, and the window is redrawn.

67
00:03:53,467 --> 00:03:55,314
Then the GPU takes over.

68
00:03:55,314 --> 00:04:01,549
Apple's GPU slices the screen into small
tiles, and renders each one inside fast,

69
00:04:01,549 --> 00:04:02,704
on-chip memory.

70
00:04:03,133 --> 00:04:07,961
Finally, the display engine streams
the finished frame to the screen,

71
00:04:07,961 --> 00:04:09,710
up to 120 times a second.

72
00:04:09,710 --> 00:04:14,888
There, millions of red, green, and
blue subpixels turn numbers into light.

73
00:04:15,467 --> 00:04:16,731
And the letter appears.

74
00:04:16,731 --> 00:04:20,907
Up through the stack, and back down
again — in a few hundredths of a second.

75
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:26,602
Typing is one journey.

76
00:04:26,602 --> 00:04:28,172
Now, let's open an app.

77
00:04:28,633 --> 00:04:33,367
Your click asks the system to launch it,
and the kernel creates a new process:

78
00:04:33,367 --> 00:04:36,463
a running program, with a
private world of its own.

79
00:04:36,867 --> 00:04:40,232
Every process gets its
own virtual address space:

80
00:04:40,232 --> 00:04:44,696
a vast map of memory that the
program believes it owns, entirely.

81
00:04:45,133 --> 00:04:49,302
But real, physical memory is
shared by every app at once.

82
00:04:49,302 --> 00:04:54,934
Hardware called the MMU translates every
virtual address into a physical one,

83
00:04:54,934 --> 00:04:55,885
page by page.

84
00:04:55,885 --> 00:04:59,469
On Apple silicon, each
page is sixteen kilobytes.

85
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:03,771
At first, most of the app's
code isn't even in memory.

86
00:05:03,771 --> 00:05:07,499
The moment it's needed, the
MMU raises a page fault.

87
00:05:07,499 --> 00:05:12,947
The kernel quietly loads that page from
the SSD, and the program carries on,

88
00:05:12,947 --> 00:05:14,023
none the wiser.

89
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:18,148
Meanwhile, the scheduler
decides which threads run where.

90
00:05:18,148 --> 00:05:22,436
Urgent work, like responding to
you, goes to the performance cores.

91
00:05:22,436 --> 00:05:25,316
Background chores go to
the efficiency cores.

92
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:28,868
And it switches between them
thousands of times a second.

93
00:05:28,868 --> 00:05:31,759
That's how hundreds of
programs seem to run at once.

94
00:05:35,567 --> 00:05:37,167
Now, press Command-S.

95
00:05:37,667 --> 00:05:39,685
Your app wants to write to storage.

96
00:05:39,685 --> 00:05:42,050
But apps aren't allowed to touch storage.

97
00:05:42,050 --> 00:05:44,472
So it asks the kernel, with a system call.

98
00:05:44,867 --> 00:05:48,563
On Apple silicon, that's one
special instruction: SVC.

99
00:05:48,563 --> 00:05:52,396
The call's number goes in a
register — four, for write —

100
00:05:52,396 --> 00:05:56,365
and the processor leaps across
the wall, into kernel mode.

101
00:05:56,833 --> 00:06:00,694
The kernel checks the request
and hands it to the file system,

102
00:06:00,694 --> 00:06:03,246
which decides where the data should live.

103
00:06:03,246 --> 00:06:08,289
Soon after, the storage controller built
into the chip writes it to flash memory.

104
00:06:08,733 --> 00:06:12,035
Flash is made of hundreds
of billions of tiny cells,

105
00:06:12,035 --> 00:06:14,067
stacked in layers hundreds high.

106
00:06:14,467 --> 00:06:17,345
Each cell stores data
as trapped electrons.

107
00:06:17,345 --> 00:06:21,763
Force electrons into its insulating
layer, and its voltage shifts.

108
00:06:21,763 --> 00:06:26,115
By telling eight levels apart, a
single cell can hold three bits.

109
00:06:26,567 --> 00:06:30,599
Those electrons can stay trapped
for years, even with the power off.

110
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:35,073
Then the kernel returns, the
processor drops back into user mode,

111
00:06:35,073 --> 00:06:36,540
and your app carries on.

112
00:06:40,333 --> 00:06:45,070
Every step we've seen — in the kernel,
the WindowServer, your app — is just code.

113
00:06:45,070 --> 00:06:46,883
But what is code, to a machine?

114
00:06:47,367 --> 00:06:50,030
Developers write in languages like Swift.

115
00:06:50,030 --> 00:06:53,212
Here's a tiny function
that adds one to a number.

116
00:06:53,633 --> 00:06:59,212
A compiler translates it, step by step,
into the processor's native language:

117
00:06:59,212 --> 00:07:00,588
ARM64 instructions.

118
00:07:01,033 --> 00:07:03,801
This one means: add one to register x0.

119
00:07:03,801 --> 00:07:08,628
And to the processor, it's just a
number: thirty-two ones and zeros.

120
00:07:09,067 --> 00:07:11,059
Every group of bits has a job.

121
00:07:11,059 --> 00:07:13,382
This one picks sixty-four-bit math.

122
00:07:13,382 --> 00:07:15,109
These say: add a constant.

123
00:07:15,109 --> 00:07:17,632
These twelve bits hold the number one.

124
00:07:17,632 --> 00:07:20,885
And these choose the
registers to read and write.

125
00:07:21,333 --> 00:07:23,864
Stored in memory, it's just four bytes.

126
00:07:23,864 --> 00:07:29,315
The core fetches them, and its decoder turns
those bits into control signals: wires,

127
00:07:29,315 --> 00:07:30,677
switching on and off.

128
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:34,012
Let's step inside a performance core.

129
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:37,387
It works like a
hyper-efficient assembly line.

130
00:07:37,387 --> 00:07:39,400
Fetch instructions from memory.

131
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:41,803
Decode them, eight or more at a time.

132
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:45,656
Then, something remarkable:
it runs them out of order.

133
00:07:45,656 --> 00:07:48,216
Hundreds of instructions wait in flight,

134
00:07:48,216 --> 00:07:51,544
and each one fires the
instant its inputs are ready.

135
00:07:51,967 --> 00:07:56,255
The results are put back in the original
order, so the program never knows.

136
00:07:56,700 --> 00:07:59,380
Our add lands in an arithmetic logic unit,

137
00:07:59,380 --> 00:08:03,719
which adds two sixty-four-bit numbers
in a single tick of the clock.

138
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:09,273
Inside is an adder: one simple
circuit, repeated for every bit.

139
00:08:09,667 --> 00:08:11,275
Each copy is a full adder.

140
00:08:11,275 --> 00:08:14,369
It takes two bits, plus a
carry from its neighbor.

141
00:08:14,369 --> 00:08:18,947
One plus one makes zero — carry the
one — just like you learned in school.

142
00:08:19,400 --> 00:08:23,624
Real chips use clever tricks to
compute all the carries in parallel.

143
00:08:23,624 --> 00:08:27,165
But the building blocks are
always the same: logic gates.

144
00:08:27,633 --> 00:08:29,112
There's the AND gate.

145
00:08:29,112 --> 00:08:29,957
The OR gate.

146
00:08:29,957 --> 00:08:30,872
Exclusive OR.

147
00:08:30,872 --> 00:08:33,407
And the most versatile of all: NAND.

148
00:08:33,407 --> 00:08:37,703
Every other logic circuit can
be built from NAND gates alone.

149
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:41,268
And every gate is built from transistors.

150
00:08:41,667 --> 00:08:43,711
This NAND gate takes just four.

151
00:08:43,711 --> 00:08:45,821
Two connect the output to power.

152
00:08:45,821 --> 00:08:47,469
Two connect it to ground.

153
00:08:47,867 --> 00:08:53,008
When both inputs turn on, the path to
ground opens, and the output falls to zero.

154
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:55,059
So what is a transistor?

155
00:08:55,059 --> 00:08:57,073
A switch, with no moving parts.

156
00:08:57,073 --> 00:09:00,647
A voltage on its gate decides
whether current can flow.

157
00:09:02,067 --> 00:09:05,117
Here's what one looks like
inside a three-nanometer chip.

158
00:09:05,633 --> 00:09:09,100
A thin fin of silicon links
the source to the drain.

159
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:14,700
Draped over it is the gate, separated by
an insulating layer only a few atoms thick.

160
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:18,752
With no voltage on the gate,
almost no current can flow.

161
00:09:18,752 --> 00:09:19,893
The switch is off.

162
00:09:20,367 --> 00:09:21,773
Apply less than a volt,

163
00:09:21,773 --> 00:09:26,724
and the gate's electric field pulls
electrons up into the fin, forming a channel.

164
00:09:27,133 --> 00:09:29,821
Now electrons stream from source to drain.

165
00:09:29,821 --> 00:09:30,909
The switch is on.

166
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:34,227
These fins are just a
few dozen atoms wide.

167
00:09:34,227 --> 00:09:39,491
And about twenty-eight billion of these
switches fit on a sliver of silicon smaller

168
00:09:39,491 --> 00:09:40,823
than a postage stamp.

169
00:09:42,233 --> 00:09:45,108
Each switch moves only a
few thousand electrons,

170
00:09:45,108 --> 00:09:48,761
and they cross the channel in
under a trillionth of a second.

171
00:09:49,267 --> 00:09:51,282
And all of it marches to a beat.

172
00:09:51,282 --> 00:09:54,747
A quartz crystal vibrates
millions of times per second.

173
00:09:54,747 --> 00:09:59,724
Circuits called phase-locked loops multiply
that into a clock of more than four

174
00:09:59,724 --> 00:10:01,299
billion ticks per second.

175
00:10:01,733 --> 00:10:06,668
At that pace, in a single tick, even light
travels only about seven centimeters —

176
00:10:06,668 --> 00:10:08,923
less than the width of a credit card.

177
00:10:09,533 --> 00:10:10,195
Every add.

178
00:10:10,195 --> 00:10:11,254
Every interrupt.

179
00:10:11,254 --> 00:10:13,041
Every pixel of that letter.

180
00:10:13,041 --> 00:10:16,548
It all happens in these
ticks — billions of switches,

181
00:10:16,548 --> 00:10:18,600
moving in perfect choreography.

182
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:22,100
So, let's rise back up.

183
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:24,852
Electrons, shaped into switches.

184
00:10:24,852 --> 00:10:26,330
Switches, into gates.

185
00:10:26,330 --> 00:10:28,370
Gates, into adders and cores.

186
00:10:28,370 --> 00:10:30,059
Cores, running a kernel.

187
00:10:30,059 --> 00:10:32,029
A kernel, serving your apps.

188
00:10:32,533 --> 00:10:35,029
Every layer hides the
complexity beneath it,

189
00:10:35,029 --> 00:10:37,525
so the layer above can
do something greater.

190
00:10:38,033 --> 00:10:42,599
That's how a sliver of purified sand
becomes a machine that answers your touch.

191
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:48,852
Next time, we'll follow the light — and
see how the GPU paints millions of pixels,

192
00:10:48,852 --> 00:10:50,092
every single frame.
